#Objective
To strengthen Linux system security by :
•	applying secure permissions,
•	protecting sensitive files,
•	limiting unauthorized access,
•	implementing the principle of least privilege.


#Context
In professional environments :
•	Incorrect permissions represent a critical risk,
•	sensitive files can be exposed,
•	excessive access increases the attack surface.
System administrators therefore implement :
•	restrictive permissions,
•	dedicated groups,
•	access controls,
•	regular audits.


#Security Policy
| Resource        | Policy                    |
| --------------- | ------------------------- |
| Sensitive Files | Restricted                |
| Backup Files    | Protected                 |
| Scripts         | Executable only if needed |
| SSH Keys        | Owner only                |
| Logs            | Controlled access         |


#Create security lab
mkdir -p ~/security-hardening-lab
cd ~/security-hardening-lab
touch confidential.txt
touch internal.txt
touch public.txt
ls -l


#Apply Secure Permissions
chmod 600 confidential.txt
chmod 640 internal.txt
chmod 644 public.txt
ls -l


#Secure Script Permissions
ls -l ~/linux-admin-toolkit/scripts
chmod 750 ~/linux-admin-toolkit/scripts/*.sh
chmod 750 ~/linux-admin-toolkit/scripts/monitoring/*.sh
chmod 750 ~/linux-admin-toolkit/scripts/user-management/*.sh
find ~/linux-admin-toolkit/scripts -type f -exec ls -l {} \;


#Secure SSH Directory
ls -ld ~/.ssh
ls -l ~/.ssh
chmod 700 ~/.ssh
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_rsa
chmod 644 ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub
ls -l ~/.ssh


#Secure Backup Files
ls -lh /opt/backups
chmod 600 /opt/backups/*.tar.gz
ls -lh /opt/backups


#Find Insecure Permissions
find ~/linux-admin-toolkit -type f -perm -o+w
find ~/linux-admin-toolkit -type d -perm -o+w

